In France, voting follows a low-tech tradition: voters separate themselves in a booth, place the pre-printed paper ballot showing their chosen candidate into an envelope.
Officials, after confirming the voter's identity, drop the voter's ballot into the ballot box and sign the voting slip.
French election law strictly codifies these processes.
Since 1988, it has been required that ballot boxes be transparent, so that voters and observers can witness that there are no converts at the beginning and that no converts are added other than those properly counted and authorized voters.
Candidates may send their representatives to be present at all stages of the process. In the evening, votes are counted under strict supervision, in accordance with special regulations, by electors.
